Here is a clean, structural representation of how the Linux kernel (iptables) processes a packet hitting your anihpj-svc Service at 10.96.45.2:80, balancing it across your running pods.

🗺️ The iptables Packet Lifecycle Table
Step
Chain / Table
Rule Match Criteria
Action Taken (-j)
System Meaning in Simple Words
1
KUBE-SERVICES
Destination IP: 10.96.45.2
Target Port: 80 (TCP)
Jump to KUBE-SVC-ANIHPJ
The Entry Gate: "Is this packet trying to talk to the anihpj-svc Service? Yes. Send it to the director."
2
KUBE-SVC-ANIHPJ
Random Mode
Probability: 25%
Jump to KUBE-SEP-POD1
Load Balancing (Pod 1): Roll a 4-sided die. If it lands on 1, route directly to the Pod 1 tracker chain.
3
KUBE-SVC-ANIHPJ
Random Mode
Probability: 33%
Jump to KUBE-SEP-POD2
Load Balancing (Pod 2): Roll a 3-sided die for the remaining traffic. If it matches, send to Pod 2.
4
KUBE-SVC-ANIHPJ
Random Mode
Probability: 50%
Jump to KUBE-SEP-POD3
Load Balancing (Pod 3): Flip a coin for the leftovers. If heads, send to Pod 3.
5
KUBE-SVC-ANIHPJ
Catch-all (No conditions left)
Jump to KUBE-SEP-POD4
Load Balancing (Pod 4): Send 100% of whatever traffic remains directly to Pod 4.
6
KUBE-SEP-PODX (Any Chosen Pod)
Native TCP packet
DNAT (Destination NAT)
The Execution: Force-rewrite the packet destination to the target container IP and application port 8000.


